  "query_preview": "..\n(Monday night through Friday)\nIssued at 1019 PM CDT Fri Oct 30 2026\n\nAs the longwave trough axis and associated cold front moves east \ntowards the Mississippi Valley area Monday night into Tuesday, \nthe upper ridge should continue to amplify. In fact, upper level \npressure patterns in models are suggestive of a weak rex block \ntype of pattern setting up over the Midwest. This could end up \nslowing this cold front down more in future guidance if this \npattern more fully develops. \n\nStrong southerly flow and a very amplified upper ridge should \nallow for highs to climb back into the mid 70s (possibly mid to \nupper 70s again). This strengthening southerly flow should help to\nbring more moisture northward from the Gulf of America towards \nmidweek. Very strong mid/low level forcing is shown in guidance \nahead of this storm system. \n\nCurrently, most guidance develops widespread and much heavier \nrainfall over northern Alabama late Tuesday night into Wednesday \nnight ahead of this stronger cold front. 0-6 km Bulk Shear is not\nextreme, but does climb to between 40 and 60 knots as the front \napproaches. In addition, portions of northern Alabama might \nrealize very meager surface based instability, so we may be \nlooking a QLCS type event shaping up sometime during that window.\nHowever, more certainty looks to be in a very heavy rainfall \nthreat (maybe 2-4 inches mainly Wednesday into Wednesday night) \ngiven the strong/deep forcing guidance is showing over the area \nand high PWATS values. Expect this heavy rainfall threat to taper \noff early Thursday morning. Wednesday looks to be a real washout, \nso highs will likely be kept in the upper 60s/lower 70s (which \nmight be generous). \n\nExpect dry conditions to return to the area late Thursday night\ninto Friday morning. Though it will be cooler behind this front,\nthe airmass is not very cold. Highs will remain cool though and\ndrop into the mid to upper 60s on Friday with some lingering cloud\ncover. Lows should slow drop into the  40 to lower 50s at night.",
